---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-drops-vs-capsules`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D Drops vs Capsules vs Softgels',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Drops vs Capsules', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D Drops vs Capsules vs Sprays: Absorption, Cost, and When to Choose Each"
  description="Head-to-head vitamin D formulations — softgels, tablets, liquid drops, chewables, sublingual sprays. Which absorbs better, what to give infants, and cost per IU."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-drops-vs-capsules", label: "Drops vs Capsules" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D Drops vs Capsules vs Sprays</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin D comes in more forms than any other over-the-counter vitamin — oil-based
      softgels, tablets, liquid drops, chewables, gummies, sublingual sprays. The
      absorption is broadly similar between properly formulated products; the differences
      that matter are compliance, dose accuracy, and cost.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Softgels (oil-based)</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      The gold standard. Vitamin D is a fat-soluble molecule and dissolves cleanly in
      olive, MCT, or safflower oil. Absorption is 60–80% when taken with a meal
      containing 10+ g of fat. Cheapest per IU. Sizes range from 400 IU to 50,000 IU.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tablets (dry-form)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D adsorbed onto cellulose or lactose. Slightly less bioavailable than
      oil-based forms per Grossmann 2010 (~ 15% less), but the difference matters mainly
      for very low fat meals. Cheap; long shelf life; good for people who can't swallow
      softgels.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Liquid drops</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Vitamin D dissolved in MCT or olive oil. Widely used for infants (400 IU/drop
      typical). Also useful for adults with dysphagia, swallowing difficulty, or those
      who want fine dose titration. Read the label carefully — potency per drop varies
      widely (some are 400 IU/drop, others are 2,000 IU/drop). One extra drop of a
      concentrated formulation is a substantial dose.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Chewables and gummies</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Good for compliance in children and adults who dislike swallowing pills. Gummies
      often contain added sugar, gelatin, and colouring — a downside for daily use.
      Potency varies more than encapsulated forms; check third-party testing.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Sublingual sprays</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Marketing promotes "faster absorption" and "bypasses digestion." In reality,
      sublingual absorption of vitamin D is limited (Todd 2016 head-to-head showed no
      significant advantage over oral). Sprays are convenient but generally more
      expensive per IU. Reasonable for people with GI malabsorption for whom oral doesn't
      raise 25(OH)D adequately.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">D3 vs D2</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Cholecalciferol (D3) raises 25(OH)D more than ergocalciferol (D2) at equivalent
      doses. Prefer D3 unless you are vegan and cannot source lichen-derived D3 (lichen
      D3 is available; most sheep-lanolin D3 is not vegan). See our
      <a href="/vitamin-d-vs-vitamin-d3" class="text-amber-700 underline">D vs D3 page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cost per 1,000 IU (approximate US 2026)</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Bulk softgels (Kirkland, Nature Made, Costco): $0.005–$0.02 per 1,000 IU</li>
      <li>Chewables: $0.03–$0.06 per 1,000 IU</li>
      <li>Standard drops: $0.02–$0.10 per 1,000 IU</li>
      <li>Sprays: $0.15–$0.40 per 1,000 IU</li>
      <li>Prescription 50,000 IU capsules: often free with insurance, otherwise ~$0.10 per 1,000 IU</li>
    </ul>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Buy from brands that participate in third-party
      testing (USP, NSF, ConsumerLab). Content assays of vitamin D supplements
      historically show ± 25% deviation from label claim in ~30% of tested products.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-drops-vs-capsules" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
